>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `enable_split_attribute`

``` hol4
splitLib.enable_split_attribute : unit -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explicitly enables the persistent `split` attribute and theorem set.

Call this before using `Theorem name[split]: ...`, or before importing
split-bearing theories whose data must replay. Loading `splitLib` or
`simpLib` alone claims neither namespace. Initialization does not depend
on `simpLib`.

Repeated calls preserve the library's existing registration and rules.
Ownership and the data handle follow `Context.restore`. If either registry
has been replaced by a foreign owner, initialization fails rather than
replacing it. `splitLib.split_thms ()` and `named_split_thms ()` return
empty lists before initialization or under foreign ownership, without
registering anything or adopting foreign data.

Pure split conversions/tactics, local `Split`/`add_split` rules, and
TypeBase-driven `split_ss` do not need initialization.

### Failure

Fails before mutation if another owner has claimed either namespace, or
if `split` is reserved/an abbreviation. Also rejects foreign listeners
under its theory-data hook names, and new initialization inside a pinned
context (an already-owned registration is still a no-op).
Malformed rules supplied later to the attribute are rejected separately.

### Comments

For a fresh import, explicit initialization must execute before the
imported theory's data replay. An exporting script's call is not itself
serialized as import-time initialization. Arrange an explicit loader
prelude/dependency; merely depending on `simpLib` does not opt in. See
`src/simp/compatibility/SPLIT_REGISTRATION.md` for the replay ordering
contract and the integration work scoped to TASK_11.

### See also

[`simpLib.split_ss`](#simpLib.split_ss),
[`splitLib.SPLIT_TAC`](#splitLib.SPLIT_TAC),
[`simpLib.Split`](#simpLib.Split).
